.\"	@(#)getc.3s	6.2 (Berkeley) 5/14/86
.\"
.TH GETC 3P "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W ANSI C
.SH NAME
getc, getchar, fgetc \- get character from stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int getc(FILE *\fIstream\fR);
.PP
.B int getchar(void);
.PP 
.B int fgetc(FILE *\fIstream\fR);
.SH (ALSO AVAILABLE IN BSD)
.PP
.B int getw(FILE *\fIstream\fR);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Getc
returns the next character from the named input
.IR stream .
.PP
.I Getchar
is identical to 
.IR getc ( stdin ).
.PP
.I Fgetc
behaves like 
.IR getc ,
but is a genuine function, not a macro;
it may be used to save object text.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
.IR Getc , 
.IR getchar , 
and
.I fgetc 
return the next character from the input stream.  If
the stream is at end-of-file, the end-of-file indicator for the 
stream is set then EOF is returned.  If a read error occurs,
the error indicator for the stream is set and EOF is
returned.
.PP
.I Getw
returns the next
.B int
from the named input
.IR stream .
It returns the constant
.SM
.B EOF
upon end of file or error, but since that is a good
integer value,
.I feof
and
.IR  ferror (3S)
should be used to check the success of
.IR getw .
.I Getw
assumes no special alignment in the file.
.I Getw
is 
.I not
an ANSI C function
(nor is it POSIX-compliant).
.SH "ERRORS"
The underlying function of 
.I getc,
.I getchar,
and
.I fgetc
is \fIread\fR(2P).
The error conditions specified for 
.I read (2P) 
apply to 
.I getc,
.I getchar,
and 
.I fgetc.
.SH BUGS
Because it is implemented as a macro,
.I getc
treats a
.I stream
argument with side effects incorrectly.
In particular,
`getc(*f++);'
doesn't work sensibly.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
read(2),
clearerr(3S),
fopen(3S),
putc(3S),
gets(3S),
scanf(3S),
fread(3S),
ungetc(3S)
